(The small city garden) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(A small garden stands between two apartment buildings. For many years) Tj
T*
(it was an empty corner where people left broken chairs. One spring, a) Tj
T*
(group of neighbours decided to change it. They cleared the ground,) Tj
T*
(collected wooden boxes, and planted herbs that could survive the) Tj
T*
(summer heat. Nobody expected the garden to feed the whole) Tj
T*
(neighbourhood. They simply wanted a useful place to meet.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The project taught the group to work with limited resources. Rainwater) Tj
T*
(was stored in covered containers, while fallen leaves became compost.) Tj
T*
(Volunteers took turns watering the plants. When an early plan failed,) Tj
T*
(they discussed what had happened and tried a different approach. The) Tj
T*
(most valuable resource was not money, but the time that people were) Tj
T*
(willing to share.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(By autumn, the garden had become a place for conversation. Children) Tj
T*
(watched insects, older residents exchanged advice, and new neighbours) Tj
T*
(learned one another's names. The harvest was modest, yet the change in) Tj
T*
(daily life was easy to see. A neglected space had become a common) Tj
T*
(responsibility.) Tj
T*
() Tj
